    why: ["Rhetoric is the art of using language to persuade, inform, influence or motivate an audience.", "It matters because it shapes opinions, changes minds and can create social or political change.", "Strong rhetoric helped people like Galileo, Harriet Beecher Stowe, Rachel Carson and Malala Yousafzai challenge powerful ideas and inspire change."],
    analyze: ["Identify the claim (the main argument).", "Determine the SOAPSTone: speaker, occasion, audience, purpose, subject, tone.", "Examine how the author persuades: ethos, pathos, logos, evidence, rhetorical devices, counterarguments and fallacies."],
    appeals: [["Ethos (credibility)", "Makes readers trust the speaker, through expertise, authority, experience or shared values.", "Why should I believe this person?"], ["Pathos (emotion)", "Appeals to feelings such as sympathy, fear, anger, pride or hope, often with vivid language, stories and imagery.", "How does this make me feel?"], ["Logos (logic)", "Appeals to reason and evidence: facts, statistics, research and clear reasoning.", "Does this make sense?"]],
    evidence: [["Anecdote", "A short personal story. Makes the argument personal and builds connection and emotion."], ["Facts", "Provable information. Builds credibility and trust; strong logos evidence."], ["Expert opinion", "Information from specialists. Readers trust trained experts more than personal opinions."], ["Data and statistics", "Numerical evidence. Persuasive, but numbers can be manipulated, so always consider the source."]],
